A||||12||Blaster||Looking to solve a problem.||||1||
Z||000000||Blaster||06-20-2000||01:53 AM||||Hello Everyone.  I am new to this Forum.  I have been reading it for a little while, but this is my first post.  Anywayz... I own a '86 se with a rebuilt 2.8.  The problem that I am having is that when I hit around 70 mph on the highway, the car begins to get a vibration from front to back in about 5 second intervals.  I had new tires put on the car when I got it, balanced and all that, so I don't think it is that. Any help would be appreciated.  Although it can't be tested out for some time since I have my cradle pulled due to some needed tranny work and a broken manifold.  But hey, the cars my baby. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>                      Thanks,<BR>                      Blaster<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Linx||06-20-2000||02:50 AM||||Well- Im still gonna say its a balance/alignment thing.  There's balance, and there's <I>balance</I>;  it aint rocket science, but then again, some places just arent too dedicated to doing it exactly right.<P>Tires can slip on a wheel or maybe you've got an sloppy wheel bearing.  The fact that it manifests itself at 70, and at a low frequency might suggest a really subtle imperfection somewhere.<P>man- Im loaded with opinions.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||tgowens||06-20-2000||02:54 AM||||and good opinions they are -- another thing could be bad front shocks.........or a worn steering stabilizer........<P>Welcome to the Forum Blaster!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||06-20-2000||07:46 AM||||Try finding a shop that has the machine that balances tires under load... can't remember the technical name.  That will tell you how it actually rolls. I'll page my dad the tire expert, see what he has to say. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||06-20-2000||12:56 PM||||That sounds like the same problem my father used to have with his L99 280Z.<BR>     Two oscillations somewhere in your car differ by a very small frequency and come in and out of phase with each other every five seconds.<BR>     How long have you been driving it since you put the new tires on? If two tires are worn differently, thus have different heights and different rolling speeds and are each out of balance slightly, they could cause somthing like what you describe.<BR>     It might also be a wheel bearing<BR>     It might be a ball joint. My car does something a little bit like that and it needs lower ball joints. How regular is this vibration? How consistent are the five second intervals?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gold-86SE||06-20-2000||02:28 PM||||<BR>Keeping with the wheels being the culprit, check this.<P>With the car on the ground, place your hands on the wheel assembly and rock it (left hand in the 9 o'clock position and your right in the 3 o'clock position).<P>Next, lift each corner of the Fiero and this time place your hands in the 12 and 6 o'clock positions and rock the wheel.<P>A worn part will manifest itself in wheel movement.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Blaster||06-20-2000||02:35 PM||||Actually, when I got the car, it had been kinda trashed.  It had been sitting for awhile due to blown head gaskets.  After that was fixed, I went and got new tires on it because the old ones had flat spots.  So I haven't rode in the car with any other tires.  I didn't think it was the tires because I thought that if it would be out of balance that it would tend to do it at other speeds.  It happens every time I take the car to about 70 in 5 second intervals, like waves through the car. You can feel it in the front of the car, then the steering wheel starts to shake and you can feel it in the floor board.  Then you feel it pass through the seat and the back of the car.<P>Thanks for the quick replies and help so far!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fret Naught||06-20-2000||02:52 PM||||My vote is tires or wheels, were they balanced using a spin balancer or static? My similar problem was a slightly bent rim, only brought to my attention because my buddy "owns a shop" was spin balancing for me and he spotted the wobble. New wheels, end of problem. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gold-86SE||06-20-2000||04:33 PM||||<BR>Additional $.02<P>If the car was "kinda trashed and sat for awhile" then pull the front wheels and repack those front bearings, you may even need new bearings.<P>Anyhow, pull the wheels, pull the rotors, clean and pack the bearings, at the minimum, this may help.<P>Also, check the condition of the cv boots at the rear, one or more may be broken. If the boots are broken, it is a strong possiblity that there is damge to the cv joint itself.  If these are bad, you will need a rebuild of the joint(s) or a new half shaft.<P>If you are not familiar with the engine/tranny setup, each half shaft has two cv joints, one toward the engine, one toward the wheel.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||06-20-2000||05:06 PM||||Sounds like you've got some worn suspension parts somewhere,also,tires will seat in ,or move sightly on the rim after being driven for about a week,so before you have them dynamically balanced,maybe just re-balance them.Cv's will rumble around when they're shot,sometimes on,and off again.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Big O||06-21-2000||12:21 AM||||The balancer Stimpy is referring to is Hunter's new Road Force Balancer.  It loads the tire during the spin cycle to check for hard spots in the tire, out of round tires, bent rims and a lot of other tire defects.  It is a really nice piece of equipment.  If the vibration was coming only from the front or back I would suspect a balance or tire defest problem.  One question, did you install aftermarket wheels when you put on the new tires.  Many of the after market wheels are hub-centeric, that is they use the hub of the wheel rather than the lug bolts to center the wheel when it's mounted.  Some of the aftermarket wheels don't fit correctly unless you use a hub-centric spacer to match the hole machined for the hub.  When this happens the wheel is not mounted on the center of the hub and no amount of balancing willl cure the problem.  It sounds to me like you need to have the entir suspension system of your car inspected.  You may need to replace all or some of the rubber bushings in the system.  If the car sat a long time the bushings could be worn or dry rotted which will make the suspension system pretty sloppy at speeds.  Bad tie rods can also be a problem as they have a tendency to cause vibration at a given speed.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||06-21-2000||04:32 PM||||I'm assuming this started with the new wheels.<P>take all the wheels off.<P>1. make sure all the weights are new. back side weights, especially small ones, are commonly overlooked.<P>2. make sure there are no stick on weights or dirt/brakedust build up inside the wheel. All this must be removed and the wheel rebalanced.<P>3. if the wheels are still off after a rebalance have them knocked down and rotated on the rims 90-180 degrees. Also do this if the rebalance wants any weights bigger than about 2oz. (For car most tires. Big tires 3-4oz) this will usually eliminate the condition of a heazy spot in the wheel lining up with a heavy spot in the tire.<P>also, when I did tires, I never mounted single weights over 2 oz. It's to easy for a peice to break off the larger sizes. (example: If the machine said 3oz on a truck tire I'd use 2 1.5's.)||||reg||1
Z||000012||wilburt||06-21-2000||07:33 PM||||If you didn't remove your wheels yet, I would try to eliminate the engine/trans. as a possible cause of the vibration. Try and find a long road on a slight downhill. Get up to where the vibration is most evident. Shift into netural, and release the gas pedal. If the vibration stops, I'd say it possiably engine/trans. related. If it continues, perhaps anything spinning (tires, wheels, etc.) is to blame. Hoping it helps.||||reg||3
